    "body": "Yeah... so it seems very unlikely that web pages add elements to `hr`s, but more likely that they use generated content, that could be floated. Which could break if we switch to containing floats by default.\r\n\r\nSearching in httparchive:\r\n```\r\nSELECT * FROM (\r\nSELECT page, url, REGEXP_EXTRACT(LOWER(body), r'(\\bhr::?(?:before|after)\\b\\s*(?:,[^\\{\\}]+)?\\{[^\\}]*\\bfloat\\s*:\\s*(?:left|right)\\b)') AS match\r\nFROM [httparchive:har.2017_05_15_chrome_requests_bodies]\r\n) WHERE match != \"null\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nI find 21 pages that appear to use floated generated content in `hr`. Inspecting them I find a visual difference for the third match (I have only checked the first 3 so far).\r\n\r\nhttp://www.digitalremedy.com/\r\nThe `hr` under \"AdReady\" heading has different rendering if I add `hr { display: flow-root; }`.\r\n<img width=\"1269\" alt=\"with flow-root the border wraps around\" src=\"https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/244772/26577007/2c9b760e-452b-11e7-8cd3-206a58841b13.png\">\r\n<img width=\"1270\" alt=\"without flow-root the border does not wrap around\" src=\"https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/244772/26577057/5452aed8-452b-11e7-93df-629c03ec8377.png\">\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
